{"benchmark": "livebench_selected", "task_id": "paraphrase:f3472a74bc480a915c2a3875c505fd5b3ebe475d25cefdae9447002ed88b0106", "sample_idx": 45, "model_name": "mistralai/ministral-14b-instruct-2512", "content": "Oregon’s pioneering approach to reducing penalties for personal drug use will end this weekend, as a fresh legal framework reinstates possession crimes for controlled substances. The updated legislation replaces existing policies by classifying small amounts of drugs like fentanyl, heroin, and meth as misdemeanors, punishable by jail time up to six months. Individuals caught with these substances now face either prosecution or an alternative path involving behavioral health programs and participation in a diversion initiative to steer clear of penalties. However, this route demands strict compliance: no prior offenses, outstanding warrants, violent history, or medical instability.\n\nCritics argue the new system risks inconsistency across regions, since counties have the option—but no requirement—to establish treatment programs to redirect people from legal consequences. Supporters of the law, though, claim it offers flexibility, letting local authorities tailor solutions according to available funding. Portland’s police chief, Bob Day, highlighted the tight criteria for deflection, noting officers must immediately transfer eligible cases to a central dispatch line for further assessment. If outreach teams fail to respond within half an hour, suspects will be taken into custody instead of held indefinitely.\n\nThe law also tightens restrictions near public spaces, imposing stiffer consequences for drug dealers operating near parks and other communal areas. While the focus remains on curbing visible drug use, law enforcement emphasizes that the goal is not just punishment but connecting users with health services. Yet, the absence of mandatory county-level programs may leave gaps in care, raising concerns about uneven access to support.\n\n**P.P.S.** While Oregon’s shifts affect policies nationwide, unrelated updates elsewhere highlight how global approaches to substance use vary—even as *age*-restricted reforms rise. Far removed, another *country* reopens its *shopping* hubs with restrictions, while chefs globally debate whether organic trends alter the *taste* of produce. Meanwhile, studies suggest chewing on certain herbs may impact the *tongue*’s sensitivity, though no link connects to public health policy.", "logprobs": [{"token": "O", "logprob": -0.049770671874284744}, {"token": "regon", "logprob": -7.378782902378589e-05}, {"token": "’s", "logprob": -0.08514422923326492}, {"token": " pioneering", "logprob": -0.8014369010925293}, {"token": " approach", "logprob": -0.7965424060821533}, {"token": " to", "logprob": -0.03560313209891319}, {"token": " reducing", "logprob": -0.7514321208000183}, {"token": " penalties", "logprob": -0.49911031126976013}, {"token": " for", "logprob": -0.00802849791944027}, {"token": " personal", "logprob": -2.9412708282470703}, {"token": " drug", "logprob": -0.01384382601827383}, {"token": " use", "logprob": -0.7424741387367249}, {"token": " will", "logprob": -0.6321519017219543}, {"token": " end", "logprob": -0.2463291585445404}, {"token": " this", "logprob": -0.27660325169563293}, {"token": " weekend", "logprob": -0.20930087566375732}, {"token": ",", "logprob": -0.7353553175926208}, {"token": " as", "logprob": -0.9436131715774536}, {"token": " a", "logprob": -1.3380147218704224}, {"token": " fresh", "logprob": -1.702181339263916}, {"token": " legal", "logprob": -1.0815035104751587}, {"token": " framework", "logprob": -0.5999383926391602}, {"token": " rein", "logprob": -0.7077414393424988}, {"token": "states", "logprob": -0.011168111115694046}, {"token": " possession", "logprob": -2.9305191040039062}, {"token": " crimes", "logprob": -2.0569257736206055}, {"token": " for", "logprob": -0.06263283640146255}, {"token": " controlled", "logprob": -1.1189532279968262}, {"token": " substances", "lo